    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5146",
      "problem": "Use synthetic division to divide $5x^4+x^3+6x+2$ by $x+1.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the dividend is missing an $x^2$ term, so we insert a $0x^2$ in its place and carry out the process in the same way.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14208915/assets/68e6d32ae1abdb12206fb421654e044360d6b95a090c4db0abeff9ec369eee9d.svg)\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ it means that $x+1$ divides $5x^4+x^3+6x+2$ exactly and gives a quotient of $5x^3-4x^2+4x+2.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5x^4+x^3+6x+2}{x+1} = 5x^3-4x^2+4x+2 .\n$$",
